"'Diligence is said to be the best of them' (1.1). Needs a pattern for ranking." owl:sameAs "'Diligence is said to be the best of them' (1.1). Needs a pattern for ranking." .

"'Rational application of mind is said to be the best of them' (1.1). Needs a pattern for ranking." owl:sameAs "'Rational application of mind is said to be the best of them' (1.1). Needs a pattern for ranking." .

"0.1" owl:sameAs "0.1" .

"All 24 statements of what fuels and starves the five hindrances and seven awakening factors, each as a nutriment node (object + kind of attention + effect). The opening statement (1.2) anchors both lists." owl:sameAs "All 24 statements of what fuels and starves the five hindrances and seven awakening factors, each as a nutriment node (object + kind of attention + effect). The opening statement (1.2) anchors both lists." .

"All ascetics and brahmins, past, future and present, who give up the three discriminations do so by developing the seven factors (1.2-1.4, 1.7-1.9); the list (1.6). The sutta doesn't name the three discriminations, so neither does the ontology." owl:sameAs "All ascetics and brahmins, past, future and present, who give up the three discriminations do so by developing the seven factors (1.2-1.4, 1.7-1.9); the list (1.6). The sutta doesn't name the three discriminations, so neither does the ontology." .

"As they are developed the mind is freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn and ignorance, and knows it is freed (1.8-1.10). Needs the three defilements (āsava) as a list and a pattern for release." owl:sameAs "As they are developed the mind is freed from the defilements of sensuality, desire to be reborn and ignorance, and knows it is freed (1.8-1.10). Needs the three defilements (āsava) as a list and a pattern for release." .

"As they investigate principles with wisdom in this way their energy is roused up and unflagging" owl:sameAs "As they investigate principles with wisdom in this way their energy is roused up and unflagging" .

"As they live mindfully in this way they investigate, explore, and inquire into that teaching with wisdom" owl:sameAs "As they live mindfully in this way they investigate, explore, and inquire into that teaching with wisdom" .

"Both lists (1.1, 1.3-1.7; 2.1, 2.3); the hindrances weaken wisdom (1.1, 1.8); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (2.1, 2.4)." owl:sameAs "Both lists (1.1, 1.3-1.7; 2.1, 2.3); the hindrances weaken wisdom (1.1, 1.8); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (2.1, 2.4)." .

"Both lists (1.1, 1.3; 2.1, 2.3); the hindrances block wisdom and don't lead to extinguishment (1.1, 1.8); the awakening factors grow wisdom and lead to extinguishment (2.1, 2.4)." owl:sameAs "Both lists (1.1, 1.3; 2.1, 2.3); the hindrances block wisdom and don't lead to extinguishment (1.1, 1.8); the awakening factors grow wisdom and lead to extinguishment (2.1, 2.4)." .

"Both lists (2.1, 2.2; 3.1, 3.2), and the teaching: when a noble disciple pays attention, applies the mind, concentrates wholeheartedly and actively listens to the teaching, the five hindrances are absent and the seven awakening factors are fully developed (1.1, 3.4)." owl:sameAs "Both lists (2.1, 2.2; 3.1, 3.2), and the teaching: when a noble disciple pays attention, applies the mind, concentrates wholeheartedly and actively listens to the teaching, the five hindrances are absent and the seven awakening factors are fully developed (1.1, 3.4)." .

"Both lists (2.1, 2.3-2.7; 3.1, 3.3); the hindrances, parasites of the mind, weaken wisdom (2.1, 2.8; stated of each hindrance in the Pali only, 2.3-2.7); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (3.1, 3.3-3.4)." owl:sameAs "Both lists (2.1, 2.3-2.7; 3.1, 3.3); the hindrances, parasites of the mind, weaken wisdom (2.1, 2.8; stated of each hindrance in the Pali only, 2.3-2.7); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (3.1, 3.3-3.4)." .

"Developing the seven factors brings one who practices them to the complete ending of suffering (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors brings one who practices them to the complete ending of suffering (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." .

"Developing the seven factors leads solely to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening (sambodhi) and extinguishment (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors leads solely to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening (sambodhi) and extinguishment (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." .

"Developing the seven factors leads to going from the near shore to the far shore (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors leads to going from the near shore to the far shore (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." .

"Developing the seven factors leads to growth and progress (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors leads to growth and progress (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." .

"Developing the seven factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.3)." .

"Developing the seven factors slants, slopes and inclines to extinguishment (1.2, 2.4); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of a bungalow's rafters inclining to the peak (1.1; D22)." owl:sameAs "Developing the seven factors slants, slopes and inclines to extinguishment (1.2, 2.4); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of a bungalow's rafters inclining to the peak (1.1; D22)." .

"Doubt about internal things is a hindrance; and doubt about external things is also a hindrance." owl:sameAs "Doubt about internal things is a hindrance; and doubt about external things is also a hindrance." .

"Dullness is a hindrance; and drowsiness is also a hindrance." owl:sameAs "Dullness is a hindrance; and drowsiness is also a hindrance." .

"Equanimity for internal things is the awakening factor of equanimity; and equanimity for external things is also the awakening factor of equanimity." owl:sameAs "Equanimity for internal things is the awakening factor of equanimity; and equanimity for external things is also the awakening factor of equanimity." .

"Everything else. The text is not held; the pairings need checking against an edition before they can be marked verified." owl:sameAs "Everything else. The text is not held; the pairings need checking against an edition before they can be marked verified." .

"Five pairings of a jhāna factor against a hindrance (citing the Peṭakopadesa)." owl:sameAs "Five pairings of a jhāna factor against a hindrance (citing the Peṭakopadesa)." .

"Good friendship is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one with good friends can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.6). Same statements as SN 46.12, added as citations. The simile of the dawn (1.1; D22)." owl:sameAs "Good friendship is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one with good friends can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.6). Same statements as SN 46.12, added as citations. The simile of the dawn (1.1; D22)." .

"Good friendship is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one with good friends can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of the dawn as forerunner of the sunrise (1.1; D22)." owl:sameAs "Good friendship is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one with good friends can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of the dawn as forerunner of the sunrise (1.1; D22)." .

"How each heart's release (by love, compassion, rejoicing, equanimity) is developed: each awakening factor developed accompanied by that quality, with the stock formula (12.1-12.3, 13.1-13.3, 14.1-14.3, 15.1-15.3; the Pali's direction, not the English's, D21). The apex of each, for a wise mendicant who has not penetrated to a higher freedom: the beautiful; the dimension of infinite space; of infinite consciousness; of nothingness (12.8-12.9, 13.6-13.7, 14.6-14.7, 15.9-15.10)." owl:sameAs "How each heart's release (by love, compassion, rejoicing, equanimity) is developed: each awakening factor developed accompanied by that quality, with the stock formula (12.1-12.3, 13.1-13.3, 14.1-14.3, 15.1-15.3; the Pali's direction, not the English's, D21). The apex of each, for a wise mendicant who has not penetrated to a higher freedom: the beautiful; the dimension of infinite space; of infinite consciousness; of nothingness (12.8-12.9, 13.6-13.7, 14.6-14.7, 15.9-15.10)." .

"Ill will for what is internal is a hindrance; and ill will for what is external is also a hindrance." owl:sameAs "Ill will for what is internal is a hindrance; and ill will for what is external is also a hindrance." .

"Immersion while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of immersion; and immersion without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of immersion." owl:sameAs "Immersion while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of immersion; and immersion without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of immersion." .

"In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics, acquiring great and abundant good qualities." owl:sameAs "In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics, acquiring great and abundant good qualities." .

"In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics." owl:sameAs "In the same way, a mendicant develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors depending on and grounded on ethics." .

"In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment." owl:sameAs "In the same way, a mendicant who develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment." .

"In the same way, for a mendicant good friendship is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors." owl:sameAs "In the same way, for a mendicant good friendship is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors." .

"In the same way, for a mendicant rational application of mind is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors." owl:sameAs "In the same way, for a mendicant rational application of mind is the forerunner and harbinger of the arising of the seven awakening factors." .

"In the same way, the five hindrances are sustained by fuel. They depend on fuel to continue, and without fuel they don’t continue." owl:sameAs "In the same way, the five hindrances are sustained by fuel. They depend on fuel to continue, and without fuel they don’t continue." .

"In the same way, the seven awakening factors are sustained by fuel. They depend on fuel to continue, and without fuel they don’t continue." owl:sameAs "In the same way, the seven awakening factors are sustained by fuel. They depend on fuel to continue, and without fuel they don’t continue." .

"In the same way, there are these five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements." owl:sameAs "In the same way, there are these five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements." .

"In the same way, there’s a time when your heart is overcome and mired in doubt and you don’t truly understand the escape from doubt that has arisen. At that time you don’t truly know or see what is good for yourself, good for another, or good for both. Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." owl:sameAs "In the same way, there’s a time when your heart is overcome and mired in doubt and you don’t truly understand the escape from doubt that has arisen. At that time you don’t truly know or see what is good for yourself, good for another, or good for both. Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." .

"In the same way, when the mind is restless, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when the mind is restless, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity." .

"In the same way, when the mind is restless, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when the mind is restless, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture." .

"In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture." .

"In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity." .

"In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in dullness and drowsiness … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in dullness and drowsiness … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." .

"In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in ill will … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in ill will … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." .

"In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in restlessness and remorse … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in restlessness and remorse … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." .

"In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in sensual desire … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." owl:sameAs "In the same way, when your heart is overcome and mired in sensual desire … Even hymns that are long-rehearsed don’t spring to mind, let alone those that are not rehearsed." .

"Investigating, exploring, and inquiring into internal things with wisdom is the awakening factor of investigation of principles; and investigating, exploring, and inquiring into external things with wisdom is also the awakening factor of investigation of principles." owl:sameAs "Investigating, exploring, and inquiring into internal things with wisdom is the awakening factor of investigation of principles; and investigating, exploring, and inquiring into external things with wisdom is also the awakening factor of investigation of principles." .

"Irrational application of mind: the hindrances arise and grow (1.1). Rational application of mind: the awakening factors arise and are fully developed (2.1). Corrected 2026-11-15: 'grow' and 'fully developed' are now in the claims (D19)." owl:sameAs "Irrational application of mind: the hindrances arise and grow (1.1). Rational application of mind: the awakening factors arise and are fully developed (2.1). Corrected 2026-11-15: 'grow' and 'fully developed' are now in the claims (D19)." .

"Irrational application of mind: the hindrances arise and grow, the awakening factors don't arise or cease (1.1-1.6). Rational application of mind: the hindrances don't arise or are given up, the awakening factors arise and are fully developed (2.1-3.1). Corrected 2026-11-15: the results 'grow', 'given up' and 'fully developed' were described here but not in the claims (D19)." owl:sameAs "Irrational application of mind: the hindrances arise and grow, the awakening factors don't arise or cease (1.1-1.6). Rational application of mind: the hindrances don't arise or are given up, the awakening factors arise and are fully developed (2.1-3.1). Corrected 2026-11-15: the results 'grow', 'given up' and 'fully developed' were described here but not in the claims (D19)." .

"Its 12 statements of what fuels the five hindrances and seven awakening factors (2-6, 9-15). They match SN 46.51 sections 1-12 (identical English; Pali differs only in punctuation and one spelling variant), so they were added as a second citation on the same claims, not as new claims. The simile framing it, the body sustained by food, for the hindrances and for the awakening factors (1.2-1.3, 7-8, 16; D22)." owl:sameAs "Its 12 statements of what fuels the five hindrances and seven awakening factors (2-6, 9-15). They match SN 46.51 sections 1-12 (identical English; Pali differs only in punctuation and one spelling variant), so they were added as a second citation on the same claims, not as new claims. The simile framing it, the body sustained by food, for the hindrances and for the awakening factors (1.2-1.3, 7-8, 16; D22)." .

"Its whole teaching: the list of the seven awakening factors (1.1, 1.3, 1.4), as citations on the existing list." owl:sameAs "Its whole teaching: the list of the seven awakening factors (1.1, 1.3, 1.4), as citations on the existing list." .

"Kuṇḍaliya's framing (1.4-1.8). 'The benefit the Realized One lives for is the fruit of knowledge and freedom' (1.9): no pattern for what someone lives for. How each step is developed: sense restraint toward agreeable and disagreeable sights and the other senses (3.2-5.6), giving up bad conduct to develop good conduct (6.2), the observing practice of each mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5): described, not modelled beyond the lists. Kuṇḍaliya's going for refuge (9.1-9.5)." owl:sameAs "Kuṇḍaliya's framing (1.4-1.8). 'The benefit the Realized One lives for is the fruit of knowledge and freedom' (1.9): no pattern for what someone lives for. How each step is developed: sense restraint toward agreeable and disagreeable sights and the other senses (3.2-5.6), giving up bad conduct to develop good conduct (6.2), the observing practice of each mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5): described, not modelled beyond the lists. Kuṇḍaliya's going for refuge (9.1-9.5)." .

"Mahācunda to the sick Buddha, at his request: the same statement as SN 46.14 (2.1-2.4); the Buddha concurs (2.5-2.6, 3.2)." owl:sameAs "Mahācunda to the sick Buddha, at his request: the same statement as SN 46.14 (2.1-2.4); the Buddha concurs (2.5-2.6, 3.2)." .

"Mendicants, dragons grow and wax strong supported by the Himalayas, the king of mountains." owl:sameAs "Mendicants, dragons grow and wax strong supported by the Himalayas, the king of mountains." .

"Mendicants, the rafters of a bungalow all slant, slope, and incline to the peak." owl:sameAs "Mendicants, the rafters of a bungalow all slant, slope, and incline to the peak." .

"Mendicants, there are these five corruptions of native gold. When native gold is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working." owl:sameAs "Mendicants, there are these five corruptions of native gold. When native gold is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working." .

"Mendicants, this body is sustained by food. It depends on food to continue, and without food it doesn’t continue." owl:sameAs "Mendicants, this body is sustained by food. It depends on food to continue, and without food it doesn’t continue." .

"Mendicants, when a Wheel-Turning Monarch appears seven treasures appear." owl:sameAs "Mendicants, when a Wheel-Turning Monarch appears seven treasures appear." .

"Mindfulness of internal things is the awakening factor of mindfulness; and mindfulness of external things is also the awakening factor of mindfulness." owl:sameAs "Mindfulness of internal things is the awakening factor of mindfulness; and mindfulness of external things is also the awakening factor of mindfulness." .

"Most of the dialogue: wisdom and consciousness (2-6), feeling, perception and consciousness (7-9), what purified mind consciousness can know and how it is understood with the eye of wisdom (10-11), the goal of wisdom and conditions for right view (12-14), states of existence (15-17), what the first absorption is (18), the five faculties (21-22), vital forces and death vs cessation (23-25), and the releases of the heart (26-37)." owl:sameAs "Most of the dialogue: wisdom and consciousness (2-6), feeling, perception and consciousness (7-9), what purified mind consciousness can know and how it is understood with the eye of wisdom (10-11), the goal of wisdom and conditions for right view (12-14), states of existence (15-17), what the first absorption is (18), the five faculties (21-22), vital forces and death vs cessation (23-25), and the releases of the heart (26-37)." .

"Nothing doctrinal. The question (x.1) and conclusion (x.4) of each section restate the claim and are not cited separately." owl:sameAs "Nothing doctrinal. The question (x.1) and conclusion (x.4) of each section restate the claim and are not cited separately." .

"Nothing else, developed like this, leads to giving up the things that tighten the fetters like the seven factors (1.1, 2.3); the list (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.2)." owl:sameAs "Nothing else, developed like this, leads to giving up the things that tighten the fetters like the seven factors (1.1, 2.3); the list (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.2)." .

"Of exterior factors, nothing gives rise to the seven awakening factors like good friendship (1.1); one with good friends can expect to develop and cultivate them (1.2), by the stock formula (1.3-1.6). Corrected 2026-11-15: 1.2-1.6 had been called abbreviated; 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6 are untranslated in the English but given in the Pali, and 1.4-1.5 give the formula with the Pali's own abbreviation (…pe…) for the middle factors (D19; refined after the re-check audit). The chapter's summary verse (2.0-2.4) is not a teaching." owl:sameAs "Of exterior factors, nothing gives rise to the seven awakening factors like good friendship (1.1); one with good friends can expect to develop and cultivate them (1.2), by the stock formula (1.3-1.6). Corrected 2026-11-15: 1.2-1.6 had been called abbreviated; 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6 are untranslated in the English but given in the Pali, and 1.4-1.5 give the formula with the Pali's own abbreviation (…pe…) for the middle factors (D19; refined after the re-check audit). The chapter's summary verse (2.0-2.4) is not a teaching." .

"Of interior factors, nothing gives rise to the seven awakening factors like rational application of mind (1.1); one accomplished in it can expect to develop and cultivate them (1.2), by the stock formula (1.3-1.6). Corrected 2026-11-15: 1.2-1.6 had been called abbreviated; 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6 are untranslated in the English but given in the Pali, and 1.4-1.5 give the formula with the Pali's own abbreviation (…pe…) for the middle factors (D19; refined after the re-check audit)." owl:sameAs "Of interior factors, nothing gives rise to the seven awakening factors like rational application of mind (1.1); one accomplished in it can expect to develop and cultivate them (1.2), by the stock formula (1.3-1.6). Corrected 2026-11-15: 1.2-1.6 had been called abbreviated; 1.2, 1.3 and 1.6 are untranslated in the English but given in the Pali, and 1.4-1.5 give the formula with the Pali's own abbreviation (…pe…) for the middle factors (D19; refined after the re-check audit)." .

"One definition: 'Unification of the mind is immersion' (12.2). Found while checking an eval answer; it corrected decision D4, which had wrongly called this equation an interpretation." owl:sameAs "One definition: 'Unification of the mind is immersion' (12.2). Found while checking an eval answer; it corrected decision D4, which had wrongly called this equation an interpretation." .

"One segment (10.2) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning the Buddha's teaching." owl:sameAs "One segment (10.2) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning the Buddha's teaching." .

"One segment (36.2), recorded as a contested use of the word 'dhamma' and deliberately left without a sense." owl:sameAs "One segment (36.2), recorded as a contested use of the word 'dhamma' and deliberately left without a sense." .

"Only the first absorption: its five factors (19.2-19.3), and that it gives up the five hindrances and possesses those five factors (20.2-20.4)." owl:sameAs "Only the first absorption: its five factors (19.2-19.3), and that it gives up the five hindrances and possesses those five factors (20.2-20.4)." .

"Physical energy is the awakening factor of energy; and mental energy is also the awakening factor of energy." owl:sameAs "Physical energy is the awakening factor of energy; and mental energy is also the awakening factor of energy." .

"Physical tranquility is the awakening factor of tranquility; and mental tranquility is also the awakening factor of tranquility." owl:sameAs "Physical tranquility is the awakening factor of tranquility; and mental tranquility is also the awakening factor of tranquility." .

"Rapture while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of rapture; and rapture without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of rapture." owl:sameAs "Rapture while placing the mind and keeping it connected is the awakening factor of rapture; and rapture without placing the mind and keeping it connected is also the awakening factor of rapture." .

"Rational application of mind is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one accomplished in it can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of the dawn (1.1; D22)." owl:sameAs "Rational application of mind is the forerunner of the arising of the seven factors (1.2), and one accomplished in it can expect to develop them (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of the dawn (1.1; D22)." .

"Restlessness is a hindrance; and remorse is also a hindrance." owl:sameAs "Restlessness is a hindrance; and remorse is also a hindrance." .

"Sensual desire for what is internal is a hindrance; and sensual desire for what is external is also a hindrance." owl:sameAs "Sensual desire for what is internal is a hindrance; and sensual desire for what is external is also a hindrance." .

"Skillful qualities are rooted in diligence (1.1); a diligent mendicant can expect to develop the seven factors (1.2); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3)." owl:sameAs "Skillful qualities are rooted in diligence (1.1); a diligent mendicant can expect to develop the seven factors (1.2); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3)." .

"Skillful qualities are rooted in rational application of mind (1.1); one accomplished in it can expect to develop the seven factors (1.2); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3)." owl:sameAs "Skillful qualities are rooted in rational application of mind (1.1); one accomplished in it can expect to develop the seven factors (1.2); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3)." .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water enveloped with moss and aquatic plants." owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water enveloped with moss and aquatic plants." .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water stirred by the wind, quivering, swirling, and rippling." owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water stirred by the wind, quivering, swirling, and rippling." .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was cloudy, murky, and muddy, hidden in the darkness." owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was cloudy, murky, and muddy, hidden in the darkness." .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was heated by fire, boiling and bubbling." owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was heated by fire, boiling and bubbling." .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was mixed with dye such as red lac, turmeric, indigo, or rose madder." owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was mixed with dye such as red lac, turmeric, indigo, or rose madder." .

"Sāriputta names the seven awakening factors (2.1-2.4)." owl:sameAs "Sāriputta names the seven awakening factors (2.1-2.4)." .

"Sāriputta's account of his own practice: he meditates in whichever factor he wishes, morning, midday and evening; knows each as limitless and properly implemented; understands that it remains while it remains, and the reason when it subsides (2.5-2.11, repeated 3.5-3.11). The simile of a chest of garments (3.1-3.4). No pattern yet for a practitioner's account or for similes." owl:sameAs "Sāriputta's account of his own practice: he meditates in whichever factor he wishes, morning, midday and evening; knows each as limitless and properly implemented; understands that it remains while it remains, and the reason when it subsides (2.5-2.11, repeated 3.5-3.11). The simile of a chest of garments (3.1-3.4). No pattern yet for a practitioner's account or for similes." .

"Taught by Venerable Upavāṇa to Sāriputta: the seven awakening factors, properly implemented by rational application of mind, lead to living at ease (2.1-2.2, 3.3)." owl:sameAs "Taught by Venerable Upavāṇa to Sāriputta: the seven awakening factors, properly implemented by rational application of mind, lead to living at ease (2.1-2.2, 3.3)." .

"That the factors are 'noble and emancipating' (ariyā niyyānikā) (1.1, 1.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is called." owl:sameAs "That the factors are 'noble and emancipating' (ariyā niyyānikā) (1.1, 1.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is called." .

"That the factors are not obstacles, hindrances or corruptions of the mind (1.1, 1.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is or is not." owl:sameAs "That the factors are not obstacles, hindrances or corruptions of the mind (1.1, 1.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is or is not." .

"That the hindrances are obstacles and corruptions of the heart, and the awakening factors are not (1.1, 1.8, 2.1, 2.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is or is not." owl:sameAs "That the hindrances are obstacles and corruptions of the heart, and the awakening factors are not (1.1, 1.8, 2.1, 2.4). Needs a pattern for what a quality is or is not." .

"The Buddha to the sick Mahākassapa: the seven awakening factors, rightly explained, developed and cultivated, lead to direct knowledge, awakening and extinguishment (3.1-3.4)." owl:sameAs "The Buddha to the sick Mahākassapa: the seven awakening factors, rightly explained, developed and cultivated, lead to direct knowledge, awakening and extinguishment (3.1-3.4)." .

"The Buddha to the sick Mahāmoggallāna: the same statement as SN 46.14 (3.1-3.4)." owl:sameAs "The Buddha to the sick Mahāmoggallāna: the same statement as SN 46.14 (3.1-3.4)." .

"The Pali compounds 'making blind, sightless, ignorant' and 'on the side of distress' for the hindrances, and their opposites for the awakening factors (1.1, 2.1), which don't map cleanly onto a target concept; and the chapter's summary verse (3.0-3.4)." owl:sameAs "The Pali compounds 'making blind, sightless, ignorant' and 'on the side of distress' for the hindrances, and their opposites for the awakening factors (1.1, 2.1), which don't map cleanly onto a target concept; and the chapter's summary verse (3.0-3.4)." .

"The awakening factors arising in sequence, each step a condition with its own words: recollecting and thinking about the teaching gives rise to mindfulness, then investigation, energy, rapture (not of the flesh), tranquility, immersion (with bliss) and equanimity (1.9-8.1); and the seven fruits and benefits they lead to, from enlightenment early in this life to heading upstream to the Akaniṭṭha realm (9.1-9.10)." owl:sameAs "The awakening factors arising in sequence, each step a condition with its own words: recollecting and thinking about the teaching gives rise to mindfulness, then investigation, energy, rapture (not of the flesh), tranquility, immersion (with bliss) and equanimity (1.9-8.1); and the seven fruits and benefits they lead to, from enlightenment early in this life to heading upstream to the Akaniṭṭha realm (9.1-9.10)." .

"The banner simile and the recollection of the Buddha, the teaching and the Saṅgha as a remedy for fear." owl:sameAs "The banner simile and the recollection of the Buddha, the teaching and the Saṅgha as a remedy for fear." .

"The chain: as they develop each factor, craving is given up; when craving is given up, deeds are given up; when deeds are given up, suffering is given up (2.2-2.4, 2.6-2.9). Needs a pattern for chains of ending, which dependent origination will also need. Also the description of each factor as 'abundant, expansive, limitless, and free of ill will' (2.1, 2.5)." owl:sameAs "The chain: as they develop each factor, craving is given up; when craving is given up, deeds are given up; when deeds are given up, suffering is given up (2.2-2.4, 2.6-2.9). Needs a pattern for chains of ending, which dependent origination will also need. Also the description of each factor as 'abundant, expansive, limitless, and free of ill will' (2.1, 2.5)." .

"The first line of the verse ('All things are not-self') as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning any phenomenon." owl:sameAs "The first line of the verse ('All things are not-self') as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning any phenomenon." .

"The five corruptions of the mind as a list whose members are the same five qualities as the hindrances (2.1, 2.3, 2.4). The simile of the five corruptions of native gold (1.1-1.8, 2.1; D22)." owl:sameAs "The five corruptions of the mind as a list whose members are the same five qualities as the hindrances (2.1, 2.3, 2.4). The simile of the five corruptions of native gold (1.1-1.8, 2.1; D22)." .

"The five higher fetters as a list (1.2, 1.4); the seven factors should be developed for their direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing and giving up (1.6, 1.12); and the formula variants: culminating in the removal of greed, hate and delusion (1.9), having freedom from death as their culmination (1.10), and inclining to extinguishment (1.11); and the seclusion formula at 1.8, which is in the Pali but not the English of that segment (added 2026-11-15, D19). The rest of the sutta is abbreviated in the source." owl:sameAs "The five higher fetters as a list (1.2, 1.4); the seven factors should be developed for their direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing and giving up (1.6, 1.12); and the formula variants: culminating in the removal of greed, hate and delusion (1.9), having freedom from death as their culmination (1.10), and inclining to extinguishment (1.11); and the seclusion formula at 1.8, which is in the Pali but not the English of that segment (added 2026-11-15, D19). The rest of the sutta is abbreviated in the source." .

"The framing: Prince Abhaya reports Pūraṇa Kassapa's view that knowing and not knowing have no cause (1.4-1.9). (The Buddha's reply, 1.10-1.13, that both have causes and reasons, is encoded; corrected 2026-11-15.) The naming 'these are called the hindrances / awakening factors' (4.2, 7.2). Prince Abhaya's responses, including 'overcome by even a single hindrance you wouldn't truly know or see' and 'endowed with even a single awakening factor you would' (4.3-4.5, 7.3-7.6): said by the interlocutor, not taught, so not encoded (D17)." owl:sameAs "The framing: Prince Abhaya reports Pūraṇa Kassapa's view that knowing and not knowing have no cause (1.4-1.9). (The Buddha's reply, 1.10-1.13, that both have causes and reasons, is encoded; corrected 2026-11-15.) The naming 'these are called the hindrances / awakening factors' (4.2, 7.2). Prince Abhaya's responses, including 'overcome by even a single hindrance you wouldn't truly know or see' and 'endowed with even a single awakening factor you would' (4.3-4.5, 7.3-7.6): said by the interlocutor, not taught, so not encoded (D17)." .

"The fulfilment ladder (D20): sense restraint fulfils the three kinds of good conduct, which fulfil the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, which fulfil the seven awakening factors, which fulfil knowledge and freedom (2.1-2.8, with the closing line of each section: 5.7, 6.3, 7.6, 8.4). The three kinds of good conduct (6.2) and the four kinds of mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5) as lists. The stock formula (8.2-8.3)." owl:sameAs "The fulfilment ladder (D20): sense restraint fulfils the three kinds of good conduct, which fulfil the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, which fulfil the seven awakening factors, which fulfil knowledge and freedom (2.1-2.8, with the closing line of each section: 5.7, 6.3, 7.6, 8.4). The three kinds of good conduct (6.2) and the four kinds of mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5) as lists. The stock formula (8.2-8.3)." .

"The hindrances are a cause and reason for not knowing and not seeing, when the heart is overcome by them (2.3-3.5); the awakening factors are a cause and reason for knowing and seeing (5.3-6.4); the stock formula (5.3, 6.2)." owl:sameAs "The hindrances are a cause and reason for not knowing and not seeing, when the heart is overcome by them (2.3-3.5); the awakening factors are a cause and reason for knowing and seeing (5.3-6.4); the stock formula (5.3, 6.2)." .

"The list (1.1, 1.3), and the teaching: the seven awakening factors, unarisen, don't arise apart from the Holy One's training (1.1, 1.4; D23). The chapter's summary verse (2.0-2.4) lists the chapter's titles and is not teaching." owl:sameAs "The list (1.1, 1.3), and the teaching: the seven awakening factors, unarisen, don't arise apart from the Holy One's training (1.1, 1.4; D23). The chapter's summary verse (2.0-2.4) lists the chapter's titles and is not teaching." .

"The list (1.1, 1.3), and the teaching: the seven awakening factors, unarisen, don't arise apart from the appearance of a Realized One (1.1, 1.4; D23)." owl:sameAs "The list (1.1, 1.3), and the teaching: the seven awakening factors, unarisen, don't arise apart from the appearance of a Realized One (1.1, 1.4; D23)." .

"The list (2.1, 2.3); when a Realized One appears, the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear (2.1, 2.4); the comparison with a Wheel-Turning Monarch's seven treasures, as a simile (1.1-1.4; D23)." owl:sameAs "The list (2.1, 2.3); when a Realized One appears, the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear (2.1, 2.4); the comparison with a Wheel-Turning Monarch's seven treasures, as a simile (1.1-1.4; D23)." .

"The list of the seven awakening factors (1.7)." owl:sameAs "The list of the seven awakening factors (1.7)." .

"The naming: the seven awakening factors are 'the seven principles that prevent decline' (aparihāniyā dhammā) (1.1, 1.3, 1.5, 1.8). Needs a pattern for what a quality is called." owl:sameAs "The naming: the seven awakening factors are 'the seven principles that prevent decline' (aparihāniyā dhammā) (1.1, 1.3, 1.5, 1.8). Needs a pattern for what a quality is called." .

"The narrative frame and the wanderers' challenge (1.1-10.2), including their restatement of the teaching: give up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom, and spread a heart full of love, compassion, rejoicing and equanimity in every direction (3.2-3.5, 4.2-4.6, 8.2-9.5): said by the interlocutors (D17). The Buddha's counter-questions and 'they're out of their element' (11.1-11.9). The five ways of perceiving at will: the repulsive in the unrepulsive, the unrepulsive in the repulsive, and so on, and staying equanimous (12.4-12.8, 13.4-13.5, 14.4-14.5, 15.4-15.8): no pattern yet. The questions also ask each release's destination, fruit and end; only the apex is answered." owl:sameAs "The narrative frame and the wanderers' challenge (1.1-10.2), including their restatement of the teaching: give up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom, and spread a heart full of love, compassion, rejoicing and equanimity in every direction (3.2-3.5, 4.2-4.6, 8.2-9.5): said by the interlocutors (D17). The Buddha's counter-questions and 'they're out of their element' (11.1-11.9). The five ways of perceiving at will: the repulsive in the unrepulsive, the unrepulsive in the repulsive, and so on, and staying equanimous (12.4-12.8, 13.4-13.5, 14.4-14.5, 15.4-15.8): no pattern yet. The questions also ask each release's destination, fruit and end; only the apex is answered." .

"The narrative frame and the wanderers' challenge (1.1-7.2), including their restatement of the teaching as 'give up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom' (3.2, 3.4, 6.1, 6.3): said by the interlocutors (D17), and the characterisation needs a pattern. The Buddha's counter-question to put to them, and that only the Realized One, his disciple or someone who heard it from them could answer it (8.1-8.6): no pattern for claims about who can answer." owl:sameAs "The narrative frame and the wanderers' challenge (1.1-7.2), including their restatement of the teaching as 'give up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom' (3.2, 3.4, 6.1, 6.3): said by the interlocutors (D17), and the characterisation needs a pattern. The Buddha's counter-question to put to them, and that only the Realized One, his disciple or someone who heard it from them could answer it (8.1-8.6): no pattern for claims about who can answer." .

"The one line given: compassion (Pali: karuṇā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: compassion (Pali: karuṇā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: equanimity (Pali: upekkhā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: equanimity (Pali: upekkhā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: love (Pali: mettā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: love (Pali: mettā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: mindfulness of breathing (Pali: ānāpānassati), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: mindfulness of breathing (Pali: ānāpānassati), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: rejoicing (Pali: muditā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: rejoicing (Pali: muditā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of a bloated corpse (Pali: uddhumātakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of a bloated corpse (Pali: uddhumātakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of a livid corpse (Pali: vinīlakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of a livid corpse (Pali: vinīlakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of a split open corpse (Pali: vicchiddakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of a split open corpse (Pali: vicchiddakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of a worm-infested corpse (Pali: puḷavakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of a worm-infested corpse (Pali: puḷavakasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of death (Pali: maraṇasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of death (Pali: maraṇasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of dispassion (Pali: virāgasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of dispassion (Pali: virāgasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of dissatisfaction with the whole world (Pali: sabbaloke anabhiratisaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of dissatisfaction with the whole world (Pali: sabbaloke anabhiratisaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of giving up (Pali: pahānasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of giving up (Pali: pahānasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of impermanence (Pali: aniccasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of impermanence (Pali: aniccasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of not-self in suffering (Pali: dukkhe anattasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of not-self in suffering (Pali: dukkhe anattasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of repulsiveness of food (Pali: āhāre paṭikūlasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of repulsiveness of food (Pali: āhāre paṭikūlasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of suffering in impermanence (Pali: anicce dukkhasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of suffering in impermanence (Pali: anicce dukkhasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The one line given: the perception of ugliness (Pali: asubhasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." owl:sameAs "The one line given: the perception of ugliness (Pali: asubhasaññā), developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1)." .

"The perception of a skeleton is very fruitful and beneficial (1.2, 1.6), developed together with the awakening factors (1.4), with the stock formula (1.5); one of two results, enlightenment in this very life or non-return; great benefit, great sanctuary from the yoke, great inspiration and great ease." owl:sameAs "The perception of a skeleton is very fruitful and beneficial (1.2, 1.6), developed together with the awakening factors (1.4), with the stock formula (1.5); one of two results, enlightenment in this very life or non-return; great benefit, great sanctuary from the yoke, great inspiration and great ease." .

"The perception of cessation is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1, 1.5); each awakening factor is developed accompanied by it (1.3-1.4, 2.5-2.6, 3.3-3.4), with the stock formula; one of two results, enlightenment in this very life or non-return; great benefit, great sanctuary from the yoke, great inspiration and great ease." owl:sameAs "The perception of cessation is very fruitful and beneficial (1.1, 1.5); each awakening factor is developed accompanied by it (1.3-1.4, 2.5-2.6, 3.3-3.4), with the stock formula; one of two results, enlightenment in this very life or non-return; great benefit, great sanctuary from the yoke, great inspiration and great ease." .

"The rest of Dhammadinnā's answers: substantial reality, its origin, cessation and the practice leading there (2-5); grasping and the aggregates (6); substantialist view (7-8); the noble eightfold path and the three spectrums of practice (9-11); the bases, prerequisites and development of immersion (12.3-12.5); the three processes (13-15); the cessation of perception and feeling (16-21); the three feelings, their underlying tendencies and their counterparts (22-29), ending with 'extinguishment is the objective, destination, and culmination of the spiritual life' (29.15); and the Buddha's endorsement of her answers (30.4-30.5), which gives the definition its authority." owl:sameAs "The rest of Dhammadinnā's answers: substantial reality, its origin, cessation and the practice leading there (2-5); grasping and the aggregates (6); substantialist view (7-8); the noble eightfold path and the three spectrums of practice (9-11); the bases, prerequisites and development of immersion (12.3-12.5); the three processes (13-15); the cessation of perception and feeling (16-21); the three feelings, their underlying tendencies and their counterparts (22-29), ending with 'extinguishment is the objective, destination, and culmination of the spiritual life' (29.15); and the Buddha's endorsement of her answers (30.4-30.5), which gives the definition its authority." .

"The rest of the verse, and the rest of the Path chapter (Dhp 273-289)." owl:sameAs "The rest of the verse, and the rest of the Path chapter (Dhp 273-289)." .

"The result 'acquiring great and abundant good qualities' (1.4, 1.13): kept in the simile's point (D22), not modelled as an outcome. The simile itself is encoded (1.2-1.4)." owl:sameAs "The result 'acquiring great and abundant good qualities' (1.4, 1.13): kept in the simile's point (D22), not modelled as an outcome. The simile itself is encoded (1.2-1.4)." .

"The reverse teaching, when the heart is not overcome and the escape is understood (13.1-22.4), and its five similes of clear water: the ontology has no concept for the absence of a quality, so the reverse similes stay out too (D22). The consequence the brahmin asked about, long-rehearsed hymns not springing to mind (2.1-2.2, 12.4, 22.4): kept in the claims' notes and the similes' points, not modelled as a claim. That the awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances or corruptions of the mind (23.1): no pattern yet. The setting and Saṅgārava's going for refuge (1.1-1.3, 24.1-24.3)." owl:sameAs "The reverse teaching, when the heart is not overcome and the escape is understood (13.1-22.4), and its five similes of clear water: the ontology has no concept for the absence of a quality, so the reverse similes stay out too (D22). The consequence the brahmin asked about, long-rehearsed hymns not springing to mind (2.1-2.2, 12.4, 22.4): kept in the claims' notes and the similes' points, not modelled as a claim. That the awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances or corruptions of the mind (23.1): no pattern yet. The setting and Saṅgārava's going for refuge (1.1-1.3, 24.1-24.3)." .

"The setting (1.1-1.3). What a mendicant understands as they rouse up each factor: 'my mind is well freed, dullness and drowsiness eradicated, restlessness and remorse eliminated, energy roused up, mind sharply focused, not sluggish' (3.1-3.2). No pattern yet for what is known in practice; read as outcomes it would overstate the text." owl:sameAs "The setting (1.1-1.3). What a mendicant understands as they rouse up each factor: 'my mind is well freed, dullness and drowsiness eradicated, restlessness and remorse eliminated, energy roused up, mind sharply focused, not sluggish' (3.1-3.2). No pattern yet for what is known in practice; read as outcomes it would overstate the text." .

"The setting (1.1-1.4). The Buddha's affirmation (2.5-2.6). The narrative of his recovery (3.3-3.4, with the Pali-only 'in that way that illness was abandoned', 3.4): narrated, not taught." owl:sameAs "The setting (1.1-1.4). The Buddha's affirmation (2.5-2.6). The narrative of his recovery (3.3-3.4, with the Pali-only 'in that way that illness was abandoned', 3.4): narrated, not taught." .

"The setting and the challenge from wanderers of other religions (1.1-3.1); the reason given for each piece of advice, that a sluggish mind is hard or easy to stimulate and a restless one hard or easy to settle (4.3, 7.3, 10.3, 13.3, repeated after each simile at 6.3, 9.3, 12.3, 15.3): cited, but no field records it. Corrected 2026-11-15: the encoded note had claimed the reasons (D19)." owl:sameAs "The setting and the challenge from wanderers of other religions (1.1-3.1); the reason given for each piece of advice, that a sluggish mind is hard or easy to stimulate and a restless one hard or easy to settle (4.3, 7.3, 10.3, 13.3, repeated after each simile at 6.3, 9.3, 12.3, 15.3): cited, but no field records it. Corrected 2026-11-15: the encoded note had claimed the reasons (D19)." .

"The setting and the exchange about his pain (1.1-2.2). Mahākassapa's affirmation (3.5-3.6). The narrative that he recovered from the illness (4.1-4.4): narrated, not taught. The Pali links the recovery to the episode, 'in that way that illness was abandoned' (tathāpahīno, 4.4, untranslated), but it teaches nothing about the factors curing illness." owl:sameAs "The setting and the exchange about his pain (1.1-2.2). Mahākassapa's affirmation (3.5-3.6). The narrative that he recovered from the illness (4.1-4.4): narrated, not taught. The Pali links the recovery to the episode, 'in that way that illness was abandoned' (tathāpahīno, 4.4, untranslated), but it teaches nothing about the factors curing illness." .

"The setting and the exchange about his pain (1.1-2.2). Moggallāna's affirmation (3.5-3.6). The narrative of his recovery (4.1-4.4, with the Pali-only 'in that way that illness was abandoned', 4.4): narrated, not taught." owl:sameAs "The setting and the exchange about his pain (1.1-2.2). Moggallāna's affirmation (3.5-3.6). The narrative of his recovery (4.1-4.4, with the Pali-only 'in that way that illness was abandoned', 4.4): narrated, not taught." .

"The seven factors are the path for crushing Māra's army (1.1, 1.5, 1.8); the list (1.7)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors are the path for crushing Māra's army (1.1, 1.5, 1.8); the list (1.7)." .

"The seven factors are the path that helps penetration (1.1, 1.5, 1.9, 2.9); the list (1.7); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.1, 2.5)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors are the path that helps penetration (1.1, 1.5, 1.9, 2.9); the list (1.7); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.1, 2.5)." .

"The seven factors are the path that leads to the cessation of craving (1.1, 1.4, 2.3); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.2)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors are the path that leads to the cessation of craving (1.1, 1.4, 2.3); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.2)." .

"The seven factors are the path that leads to the ending of craving (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.1, 2.5)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors are the path that leads to the ending of craving (1.1, 1.4); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.1, 2.5)." .

"The seven factors developed depending on and grounded on ethics (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of creatures in the four postures depending on the earth (1.1-1.2; D22)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors developed depending on and grounded on ethics (1.3); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.3). The simile of creatures in the four postures depending on the earth (1.1-1.2; D22)." .

"The seven factors developed depending on and grounded on ethics (1.4), and the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.6, 1.12). The simile of dragons growing strong on the Himalayas (1.2-1.4; D22)." owl:sameAs "The seven factors developed depending on and grounded on ethics (1.4), and the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.6, 1.12). The simile of dragons growing strong on the Himalayas (1.2-1.4; D22)." .

"The simile of the large parasitic fig trees whose host trees collapse (1.1-1.4). Its point is a renunciate overcome by sensual pleasures like those he gave up, or worse (1.5), which is not modelled, so under D22 the simile stays out too. That the hindrances are obstacles and the awakening factors are not (2.1, 3.1): no pattern yet for what a quality is or is not (as SN 46.37)." owl:sameAs "The simile of the large parasitic fig trees whose host trees collapse (1.1-1.4). Its point is a renunciate overcome by sensual pleasures like those he gave up, or worse (1.5), which is not modelled, so under D22 the simile stays out too. That the hindrances are obstacles and the awakening factors are not (2.1, 3.1): no pattern yet for what a quality is or is not (as SN 46.37)." .

"The six sets of six (sense fields, objects, consciousnesses, contacts, feelings, cravings) and the not-self argument built on them." owl:sameAs "The six sets of six (sense fields, objects, consciousnesses, contacts, feelings, cravings) and the not-self argument built on them." .

"The timing advice: which awakening factors it is the right or wrong time to develop when the mind is sluggish (4.1, 7.1) or restless (10.1, 13.1), and 'mindfulness is always useful' (15.4). The four fire similes, each illustrating three pieces of timing advice (5-6, 8-9, 11-12, 14-15; D22)." owl:sameAs "The timing advice: which awakening factors it is the right or wrong time to develop when the mind is sluggish (4.1, 7.1) or restless (10.1, 13.1), and 'mindfulness is always useful' (15.4). The four fire similes, each illustrating three pieces of timing advice (5-6, 8-9, 11-12, 14-15; D22)." .

"The verses (2.1-6.6): few cross to the far shore, the astute develop the bright, and those rightly developed in the awakening factors are quenched in this world." owl:sameAs "The verses (2.1-6.6): few cross to the far shore, the astute develop the bright, and those rightly developed in the awakening factors are quenched in this world." .

"The way in which the five hindrances become ten (9.1-9.12) and the seven awakening factors become fourteen (10.1-16.3): each of the twelve becomes twofold, as twelve divisions (D18) on four bases: internal and external (sensual desire, ill will, doubt, mindfulness, investigation, equanimity), physical and mental (energy, tranquility), with and without placing the mind (rapture, immersion), and the two qualities a compound name joins (dullness / drowsiness, restlessness / remorse)." owl:sameAs "The way in which the five hindrances become ten (9.1-9.12) and the seven awakening factors become fourteen (10.1-16.3): each of the twelve becomes twofold, as twelve divisions (D18) on four bases: internal and external (sensual desire, ill will, doubt, mindfulness, investigation, equanimity), physical and mental (energy, tranquility), with and without placing the mind (rapture, immersion), and the two qualities a compound name joins (dullness / drowsiness, restlessness / remorse)." .

"The whole teaching on the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. Its section on the five hindrances (knowing when each is present, how it arises, how it is given up) bears directly on this ontology and should come next." owl:sameAs "The whole teaching on the four kinds of mindfulness meditation. Its section on the five hindrances (knowing when each is present, how it arises, how it is given up) bears directly on this ontology and should come next." .

"They closely watch over that mind immersed in samādhi" owl:sameAs "They closely watch over that mind immersed in samādhi" .

"This body is sustained by food. It depends on food to continue, and without food it doesn’t continue." owl:sameAs "This body is sustained by food. It depends on food to continue, and without food it doesn’t continue." .

"Two segments (5.3, 6.8) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning an object of the mind." owl:sameAs "Two segments (5.3, 6.8) as evidence for 'dhamma' meaning an object of the mind." .

"What the corruptions do: the mind is not pliable, workable or radiant, is brittle, and is not rightly immersed for the ending of defilements (2.1, 2.4): quoted in the simile's point (D22), but no pattern yet for what a quality does." owl:sameAs "What the corruptions do: the mind is not pliable, workable or radiant, is brittle, and is not rightly immersed for the ending of defilements (2.1, 2.4): quoted in the simile's point (D22), but no pattern yet for what a quality does." .

"What the things that tighten the fetters are: the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body and mind, where the fetters, shackles and attachments arise (3.1-3.8). Needs the six sense fields as a list and a pattern for 'X is where Y arises'." owl:sameAs "What the things that tighten the fetters are: the eye, ear, nose, tongue, body and mind, where the fetters, shackles and attachments arise (3.1-3.8). Needs the six sense fields as a list and a pattern for 'X is where Y arises'." .

"When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear" owl:sameAs "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear" .

"When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear." owl:sameAs "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear." .

"When the body is tranquil and one feels bliss, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi" owl:sameAs "When the body is tranquil and one feels bliss, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi" .

"When the heart is overcome by each of five (sensual lust, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, doubt) and the escape from it isn't understood, one doesn't truly know or see what is good for oneself, for another or for both (3.1, 5.1, 7.1, 9.1, 11.1); the five similes of a bowl of water that is dyed, boiling, covered with moss, stirred by the wind, or muddy in the dark (4, 6, 8, 10, 12; D22); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (23.1, 23.3-23.4)." owl:sameAs "When the heart is overcome by each of five (sensual lust, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, doubt) and the escape from it isn't understood, one doesn't truly know or see what is good for oneself, for another or for both (3.1, 5.1, 7.1, 9.1, 11.1); the five similes of a bowl of water that is dyed, boiling, covered with moss, stirred by the wind, or muddy in the dark (4, 6, 8, 10, 12; D22); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (23.1, 23.3-23.4)." .

"When the mind is full of rapture, the body and mind become tranquil" owl:sameAs "When the mind is full of rapture, the body and mind become tranquil" .

"Why it matters to see, hear, approach, pay homage to, recollect and go forth following mendicants accomplished in ethics, immersion, knowledge, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom: 'very helpful' (1.1-1.6); and living withdrawn in body and mind after hearing their teaching (1.8). Needs a pattern for what is called helpful, and the link from hearing to withdrawal is not modelled. Within the sequence, each factor is 'initiated', 'developed' and 'perfected' (e.g. 2.2-2.3); only its arising is modelled. Fruits 3-7 come 'with the ending of the five lower fetters' (9.5-9.9): kept in a note, not modelled." owl:sameAs "Why it matters to see, hear, approach, pay homage to, recollect and go forth following mendicants accomplished in ethics, immersion, knowledge, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom: 'very helpful' (1.1-1.6); and living withdrawn in body and mind after hearing their teaching (1.8). Needs a pattern for what is called helpful, and the link from hearing to withdrawal is not modelled. Within the sequence, each factor is 'initiated', 'developed' and 'perfected' (e.g. 2.2-2.3); only its arising is modelled. Fruits 3-7 come 'with the ending of the five lower fetters' (9.5-9.9): kept in a note, not modelled." .

"Why they are called awakening factors: they lead to awakening (bodhi) (1.5, 1.11); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.7)." owl:sameAs "Why they are called awakening factors: they lead to awakening (bodhi) (1.5, 1.11); the list (1.6); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (1.7)." .

"Why they are called awakening factors: they lead to awakening (bodhi) (2.3, 2.6); the list (2.4); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.5)." owl:sameAs "Why they are called awakening factors: they lead to awakening (bodhi) (2.3, 2.6); the list (2.4); the stock formula for each factor (relies on seclusion, fading away and cessation; ripens as letting go) (2.5)." .

"With a mind that has developed each factor, they penetrate and shatter the mass of greed, hate and delusion for the first time (2.2-2.4, 2.6-2.8); 'weakens' would understate 'shatter', so it waits for a better fit. Also 'abundant, expansive, limitless, and free of ill will' (2.1, 2.5)." owl:sameAs "With a mind that has developed each factor, they penetrate and shatter the mass of greed, hate and delusion for the first time (2.2-2.4, 2.6-2.8); 'weakens' would understate 'shatter', so it waits for a better fit. Also 'abundant, expansive, limitless, and free of ill will' (2.1, 2.5)." .

