"Stated as a superlative: nothing else, developed like this, does so like the seven awakening factors."@en owl:sameAs "Stated as a superlative: nothing else, developed like this, does so like the seven awakening factors."@en .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water enveloped with moss and aquatic plants."@en owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water enveloped with moss and aquatic plants."@en .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water stirred by the wind, quivering, swirling, and rippling."@en owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water stirred by the wind, quivering, swirling, and rippling."@en .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was cloudy, murky, and muddy, hidden in the darkness."@en owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was cloudy, murky, and muddy, hidden in the darkness."@en .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was heated by fire, boiling and bubbling."@en owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was heated by fire, boiling and bubbling."@en .

"Suppose there was a bowl of water that was mixed with dye such as red lac, turmeric, indigo, or rose madder."@en owl:sameAs "Suppose there was a bowl of water that was mixed with dye such as red lac, turmeric, indigo, or rose madder."@en .

"Sutta (early discourses)"@en owl:sameAs "Sutta (early discourses)"@en .

"Taught by Venerable Upavāṇa in answer to Sāriputta. 'Properly implemented' (susamāraddhā) 'by rational application of mind' (yonisomanasikārā); Sujato renders the last 'by investigating inside themselves'."@en owl:sameAs "Taught by Venerable Upavāṇa in answer to Sāriputta. 'Properly implemented' (susamāraddhā) 'by rational application of mind' (yonisomanasikārā); Sujato renders the last 'by investigating inside themselves'."@en .

"Text"@en owl:sameAs "Text"@en .

"That’s how a mendicant can know that by investigating inside themselves, the seven awakening factors are properly implemented so that they lead to living at ease.”"@en owl:sameAs "That’s how a mendicant can know that by investigating inside themselves, the seven awakening factors are properly implemented so that they lead to living at ease.”"@en .

"That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment.”"@en owl:sameAs "That’s how a mendicant who develops and cultivates the seven awakening factors slants, slopes, and inclines to extinguishment.”"@en .

"That’s how sense restraint is developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the three kinds of good conduct."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how sense restraint is developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the three kinds of good conduct."@en .

"That’s how the four kinds of mindfulness meditation are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the seven awakening factors."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the four kinds of mindfulness meditation are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the seven awakening factors."@en .

"That’s how the perception of a skeleton, when developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial.”"@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the perception of a skeleton, when developed and cultivated, is very fruitful and beneficial.”"@en .

"That’s how the perception of cessation, when developed and cultivated, leads to great benefit … great sanctuary from the yoke … great inspiration … great ease.”"@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the perception of cessation, when developed and cultivated, leads to great benefit … great sanctuary from the yoke … great inspiration … great ease.”"@en .

"That’s how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill knowledge and freedom.”"@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill knowledge and freedom.”"@en .

"That’s how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to giving up the things that tighten the fetters."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to giving up the things that tighten the fetters."@en .

"That’s how the three kinds of good conduct are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the four kinds of mindfulness meditation."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how the three kinds of good conduct are developed and cultivated so as to fulfill the four kinds of mindfulness meditation."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of energy’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of energy’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of investigation of principles’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of investigation of principles’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of mindfulness’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the awakening factor of mindfulness’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of doubt’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of doubt’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of dullness and drowsiness’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of dullness and drowsiness’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of ill will’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of ill will’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of restlessness and remorse’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of restlessness and remorse’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of sensual desire’ becomes twofold."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how what is concisely referred to as ‘the hindrance of sensual desire’ becomes twofold."@en .

"That’s how, when the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated, it’s very fruitful and beneficial."@en owl:sameAs "That’s how, when the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated, it’s very fruitful and beneficial."@en .

"The Pali of 12.8 ends 'or else they enter and remain in the liberation of the beautiful' (subhaṁ vā kho pana vimokkhaṁ upasampajja viharati); Sujato's English of that segment leaves it out."@en owl:sameAs "The Pali of 12.8 ends 'or else they enter and remain in the liberation of the beautiful' (subhaṁ vā kho pana vimokkhaṁ upasampajja viharati); Sujato's English of that segment leaves it out."@en .

"The Pali of every cited segment is abbreviated (…pe…). The claim rests on the conventional expansion from the full sutta of the series, as the translation fills it in, not on words the text itself gives (D14, D19)."@en owl:sameAs "The Pali of every cited segment is abbreviated (…pe…). The claim rests on the conventional expansion from the full sutta of the series, as the translation fills it in, not on words the text itself gives (D14, D19)."@en .

"The Pali: the mendicant develops each awakening factor accompanied by the perception of a skeleton. Sujato's English: 'develops the perception of a skeleton together with the awakening factors'."@en owl:sameAs "The Pali: the mendicant develops each awakening factor accompanied by the perception of a skeleton. Sujato's English: 'develops the perception of a skeleton together with the awakening factors'."@en .

"The Pali: the mendicant develops each awakening factor accompanied by the perception of cessation. Sujato's English: 'develops the perception of cessation together with the awakening factors'."@en owl:sameAs "The Pali: the mendicant develops each awakening factor accompanied by the perception of cessation. Sujato's English: 'develops the perception of cessation together with the awakening factors'."@en .

"The apex of the heart’s release by compassion is the dimension of infinite space, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en owl:sameAs "The apex of the heart’s release by compassion is the dimension of infinite space, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en .

"The apex of the heart’s release by equanimity is the dimension of nothingness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom.”"@en owl:sameAs "The apex of the heart’s release by equanimity is the dimension of nothingness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom.”"@en .

"The apex of the heart’s release by love is the beautiful, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en owl:sameAs "The apex of the heart’s release by love is the beautiful, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en .

"The apex of the heart’s release by rejoicing is the dimension of infinite consciousness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en owl:sameAs "The apex of the heart’s release by rejoicing is the dimension of infinite consciousness, I say, for a wise mendicant here who has not penetrated to a higher freedom."@en .

"The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in byāpāda, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en owl:sameAs "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in byāpāda, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en .

"The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in kāmarāga, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en owl:sameAs "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in kāmarāga, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en .

"The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in thinamiddha, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en owl:sameAs "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in thinamiddha, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en .

"The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in uddhaccakukkucca, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en owl:sameAs "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in uddhaccakukkucca, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind. In both cited segments the Pali gives 'for oneself' and 'for both' in full and abbreviates 'for another' (paratthampi …pe…), which rests on the conventional expansion (D19)."@en .

"The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in vicikicchā, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en owl:sameAs "The condition has two parts, and the controlled relation shows only the first: the heart overcome and mired in vicikicchā, and not truly understanding the escape from it once it has arisen (the wording keeps both). The text adds the answer to the brahmin's question: even long-rehearsed hymns don't spring to mind."@en .

"The dragons grow strong on the mountain, then go down to pools, lakes, streams, rivers and the ocean, acquiring a great and abundant body (1.3); so the mendicant, grounded on ethics, acquires great and abundant good qualities."@en owl:sameAs "The dragons grow strong on the mountain, then go down to pools, lakes, streams, rivers and the ocean, acquiring a great and abundant body (1.3); so the mendicant, grounded on ethics, acquires great and abundant good qualities."@en .

"The evidence a derived link was built from."@en owl:sameAs "The evidence a derived link was built from."@en .

"The image, verbatim from a cited passage (S17). The point ('in the same way …') is dho:wording (S14)."@en owl:sameAs "The image, verbatim from a cited passage (S17). The point ('in the same way …') is dho:wording (S14)."@en .

"The passage(s) that state this claim."@en owl:sameAs "The passage(s) that state this claim."@en .

"The same image as for good friendship (SN 46.12), with a different point, so a separate simile."@en owl:sameAs "The same image as for good friendship (SN 46.12), with a different point, so a separate simile."@en .

"The same simile, with the same point, in SN 46.12 and 46.48 (D12)."@en owl:sameAs "The same simile, with the same point, in SN 46.12 and 46.48 (D12)."@en .

"The seven awakening factors should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters."@en owl:sameAs "The seven awakening factors should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters."@en .

"The seven awakening factors should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters.”"@en owl:sameAs "The seven awakening factors should be developed for the direct knowledge, complete understanding, finishing, and giving up of these five higher fetters.”"@en .

"The teaching the Buddha explained: 'the teaching is well explained by the Buddha' (svākkhāto bhagavatā dhammo)."@en owl:sameAs "The teaching the Buddha explained: 'the teaching is well explained by the Buddha' (svākkhāto bhagavatā dhammo)."@en .

"The text sets the two side by side without 'just as … in the same way' (seyyathāpi … evameva); treated as a simile because the monarch's treasures are there only to illustrate (D23). The seven treasures are named in 1.3."@en owl:sameAs "The text sets the two side by side without 'just as … in the same way' (seyyathāpi … evameva); treated as a simile because the monarch's treasures are there only to illustrate (D23). The seven treasures are named in 1.3."@en .

"The text's order (9.3-9.9): enlightenment early in this very life; at the time of death; extinguished between one life and the next; upon landing; without extra effort; with extra effort; heading upstream to the Akaniṭṭha realm. The members are a set here; the order is in this note only."@en owl:sameAs "The text's order (9.3-9.9): enlightenment early in this very life; at the time of death; extinguished between one life and the next; upon landing; without extra effort; with extra effort; heading upstream to the Akaniṭṭha realm. The members are a set here; the order is in this note only."@en .

"The text's own words for the relation, verbatim from the cited English (Sujato). S14 checks it occurs in a cited passage."@en owl:sameAs "The text's own words for the relation, verbatim from the cited English (Sujato). S14 checks it occurs in a cited passage."@en .

"The treasures of the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en owl:sameAs "The treasures of the awakening factors of mindfulness, investigation of principles, energy, rapture, tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en .

"The wheel, the elephant, the horse, the jewel, the woman, the householder, and the commander."@en owl:sameAs "The wheel, the elephant, the horse, the jewel, the woman, the householder, and the commander."@en .

"There are causes and reasons for knowing and seeing."@en owl:sameAs "There are causes and reasons for knowing and seeing."@en .

"There are the sense fields of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and ideas."@en owl:sameAs "There are the sense fields of sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches, and ideas."@en .

"There are these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom."@en owl:sameAs "There are these five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom."@en .

"There are these seven awakening factors that are not obstacles, hindrances, or corruptions of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en owl:sameAs "There are these seven awakening factors that are not obstacles, hindrances, or corruptions of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en .

"There is the feature of ugliness."@en owl:sameAs "There is the feature of ugliness."@en .

"There is the heart’s release by love."@en owl:sameAs "There is the heart’s release by love."@en .

"There is the settled mind."@en owl:sameAs "There is the settled mind."@en .

"These are the five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements.”"@en owl:sameAs "These are the five corruptions of the mind. When the mind is corrupted by these it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant. It’s brittle, and not rightly immersed in samādhi for the ending of defilements.”"@en .

"These are the five obstacles and hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom."@en owl:sameAs "These are the five obstacles and hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom."@en .

"These are the five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom."@en owl:sameAs "These are the five obstacles and hindrances, parasites of the mind that weaken wisdom."@en .

"These are the seven awakening factors that the Buddha has rightly explained. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment.”"@en owl:sameAs "These are the seven awakening factors that the Buddha has rightly explained. When developed and cultivated, they lead to direct knowledge, to awakening, and to extinguishment.”"@en .

"These are the seven awakening factors."@en owl:sameAs "These are the seven awakening factors."@en .

"These five hindrances are destroyers of sight, vision, and knowledge. They block wisdom, they’re on the side of distress, and they don’t lead to extinguishment."@en owl:sameAs "These five hindrances are destroyers of sight, vision, and knowledge. They block wisdom, they’re on the side of distress, and they don’t lead to extinguishment."@en .

"These seven awakening factors are creators of vision and knowledge. They grow wisdom, they’re on the side of solace, and they lead to extinguishment."@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors are creators of vision and knowledge. They grow wisdom, they’re on the side of solace, and they lead to extinguishment."@en .

"These seven awakening factors are creators of vision and knowledge. They grow wisdom, they’re on the side of solace, and they lead to extinguishment.”"@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors are creators of vision and knowledge. They grow wisdom, they’re on the side of solace, and they lead to extinguishment.”"@en .

"These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or corruptions of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or corruptions of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en .

"These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or parasites of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or parasites of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom."@en .

"These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or parasites of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom.”"@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances, or parasites of the mind. When developed and cultivated they lead to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom.”"@en .

"These seven awakening factors don’t arise to be developed and cultivated apart from the Holy One’s training.”"@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors don’t arise to be developed and cultivated apart from the Holy One’s training.”"@en .

"These seven awakening factors don’t arise to be developed and cultivated except when a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha has appeared.”"@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors don’t arise to be developed and cultivated except when a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha has appeared.”"@en .

"These seven awakening factors, when developed and cultivated, lead solely to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment.”"@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors, when developed and cultivated, lead solely to disillusionment, dispassion, cessation, peace, insight, awakening, and extinguishment.”"@en .

"These seven awakening factors, when developed and cultivated, lead to going from the near shore to the far shore."@en owl:sameAs "These seven awakening factors, when developed and cultivated, lead to going from the near shore to the far shore."@en .

"They attain enlightenment early on in this very life."@en owl:sameAs "They attain enlightenment early on in this very life."@en .

"They closely watch over that mind immersed in samādhi."@en owl:sameAs "They closely watch over that mind immersed in samādhi."@en .

"They develop the awakening factor of equanimity, which relies on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripens as letting go."@en owl:sameAs "They develop the awakening factor of equanimity, which relies on seclusion, fading away, and cessation, and ripens as letting go."@en .

"They meditate observing an aspect of feelings …"@en owl:sameAs "They meditate observing an aspect of feelings …"@en .

"They truly know and see with a mind that has developed each awakening factor. Given in full for mindfulness, abbreviated for the others."@en owl:sameAs "They truly know and see with a mind that has developed each awakening factor. Given in full for mindfulness, abbreviated for the others."@en .

"They truly know and see with a mind that has developed the awakening factor of equanimity."@en owl:sameAs "They truly know and see with a mind that has developed the awakening factor of equanimity."@en .

"They truly know and see with a mind that has developed the awakening factor of mindfulness."@en owl:sameAs "They truly know and see with a mind that has developed the awakening factor of mindfulness."@en .

"This is how are the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to penetration.”"@en owl:sameAs "This is how are the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to penetration.”"@en .

"This is how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to the cessation of craving.”"@en owl:sameAs "This is how the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated so as to lead to the cessation of craving.”"@en .

"This is the path for crushing Māra’s army.”"@en owl:sameAs "This is the path for crushing Māra’s army.”"@en .

"This is the way in which the five hindrances become ten."@en owl:sameAs "This is the way in which the five hindrances become ten."@en .

"This is the way in which the seven awakening factors become fourteen.”"@en owl:sameAs "This is the way in which the seven awakening factors become fourteen.”"@en .

"Timing"@en owl:sameAs "Timing"@en .

"Timing advice"@en owl:sameAs "Timing advice"@en .

"Together with bliss: 'when the body is tranquil and one feels bliss'."@en owl:sameAs "Together with bliss: 'when the body is tranquil and one feels bliss'."@en .

"Trying to put out a great fire with dry fuel and blowing on it: the restless mind and the wrong factors."@en owl:sameAs "Trying to put out a great fire with dry fuel and blowing on it: the restless mind and the wrong factors."@en .

"Visuddhimagga IV.86"@en owl:sameAs "Visuddhimagga IV.86"@en .

"What are the five hindrances that are absent?"@en owl:sameAs "What are the five hindrances that are absent?"@en .

"What has the effect: a quality, a list, or a practice."@en owl:sameAs "What has the effect: a quality, a list, or a practice."@en .

"What seven?"@en owl:sameAs "What seven?"@en .

"What the mind is applied to, e.g. 'the feature of beauty' (subhanimitta)."@en owl:sameAs "What the mind is applied to, e.g. 'the feature of beauty' (subhanimitta)."@en .

"What the mind knows, as the sixth sense field alongside sights, sounds, smells, tastes and touches (dhammāyatana)."@en owl:sameAs "What the mind knows, as the sixth sense field alongside sights, sounds, smells, tastes and touches (dhammāyatana)."@en .

"When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear."@en owl:sameAs "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears the seven treasures of the awakening factors appear."@en .

"When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears these seven treasures of the awakening factors appear.”"@en owl:sameAs "When a Realized One, a perfected one, a fully awakened Buddha appears these seven treasures of the awakening factors appear.”"@en .

"When a Wheel-Turning Monarch appears these seven treasures appear."@en owl:sameAs "When a Wheel-Turning Monarch appears these seven treasures appear."@en .

"When a mendicant has entered the first absorption, placing the mind, keeping it connected, rapture, bliss, and unification of mind are present."@en owl:sameAs "When a mendicant has entered the first absorption, placing the mind, keeping it connected, rapture, bliss, and unification of mind are present."@en .

"When a mendicant has entered the first absorption, sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt are given up."@en owl:sameAs "When a mendicant has entered the first absorption, sensual desire, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, and doubt are given up."@en .

"When native gold is corrupted by these five corruptions it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working."@en owl:sameAs "When native gold is corrupted by these five corruptions it’s not pliable, workable, or radiant, but is brittle and not completely ready for working."@en .

"When the body is tranquil and one feels bliss, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi."@en owl:sameAs "When the body is tranquil and one feels bliss, the mind becomes immersed in samādhi."@en .

"When the heart is overcome and mired in each hindrance, without truly knowing and seeing the escape from it. Given in full for sensual desire, abbreviated for the others."@en owl:sameAs "When the heart is overcome and mired in each hindrance, without truly knowing and seeing the escape from it. Given in full for sensual desire, abbreviated for the others."@en .

"When the mind is full of rapture, the body and mind become tranquil."@en owl:sameAs "When the mind is full of rapture, the body and mind become tranquil."@en .

"When the mind is restless, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en owl:sameAs "When the mind is restless, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en .

"When the mind is restless, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture."@en owl:sameAs "When the mind is restless, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture."@en .

"When the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture."@en owl:sameAs "When the mind is sluggish, it’s the right time to develop the awakening factors of investigation of principles, energy, and rapture."@en .

"When the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en owl:sameAs "When the mind is sluggish, it’s the wrong time to develop the awakening factors of tranquility, immersion, and equanimity."@en .

"When the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated in this way you can expect one of two results:"@en owl:sameAs "When the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated in this way you can expect one of two results:"@en .

"When the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated you can expect one of two results:"@en owl:sameAs "When the perception of cessation is developed and cultivated you can expect one of two results:"@en .

"When the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated in this way these are the seven fruits and benefits they can expect.”"@en owl:sameAs "When the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated in this way these are the seven fruits and benefits they can expect.”"@en .

"When the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated in this way they can expect seven fruits and benefits."@en owl:sameAs "When the seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated in this way they can expect seven fruits and benefits."@en .

"When these seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated they are noble and emancipating, and bring one who practices them to the complete ending of suffering.”"@en owl:sameAs "When these seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated they are noble and emancipating, and bring one who practices them to the complete ending of suffering.”"@en .

"When these seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated they lead to growth and progress.”"@en owl:sameAs "When these seven awakening factors are developed and cultivated they lead to growth and progress.”"@en .

"When they’re energetic, rapture not of the flesh arises."@en owl:sameAs "When they’re energetic, rapture not of the flesh arises."@en .

"When they’re strong they dive into the pools. Then they dive into the lakes, the streams, the rivers, and finally the ocean. There they acquire a great and abundant body."@en owl:sameAs "When they’re strong they dive into the pools. Then they dive into the lakes, the streams, the rivers, and finally the ocean. There they acquire a great and abundant body."@en .

"When you apply the mind rationally, sensual desire,"@en owl:sameAs "When you apply the mind rationally, sensual desire,"@en .

"Which sense of an ambiguous word a passage uses. This is interpretation, so it records who made the call."@en owl:sameAs "Which sense of an ambiguous word a passage uses. This is interpretation, so it records who made the call."@en .

"Which source layer a text or claim belongs to."@en owl:sameAs "Which source layer a text or claim belongs to."@en .

"X-sahagata: the awakening factor is developed accompanied by X (SN 46.57-76). The condition is X, the conditioned is the awakening factors. Sujato's English turns the grammar round ('develops X together with the awakening factors'); the Pali is followed (D15 correction, D19)."@en owl:sameAs "X-sahagata: the awakening factor is developed accompanied by X (SN 46.57-76). The condition is X, the conditioned is the awakening factors. Sujato's English turns the grammar round ('develops X together with the awakening factors'); the Pali is followed (D15 correction, D19)."@en .

