dho:t-sn46.55 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.55 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.55 Saṅgāravasutta — With Saṅgārava"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:PartlyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "When the heart is overcome by each of five (sensual lust, ill will, dullness and drowsiness, restlessness and remorse, doubt) and the escape from it isn't understood, one doesn't truly know or see what is good for oneself, for another or for both (3.1, 5.1, 7.1, 9.1, 11.1); the five similes of a bowl of water that is dyed, boiling, covered with moss, stirred by the wind, or muddy in the dark (4, 6, 8, 10, 12; D22); developing the awakening factors leads to the realization of the fruit of knowledge and freedom (23.1, 23.3-23.4)." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:leftOut "The reverse teaching, when the heart is not overcome and the escape is understood (13.1-22.4), and its five similes of clear water: the ontology has no concept for the absence of a quality, so the reverse similes stay out too (D22). The consequence the brahmin asked about, long-rehearsed hymns not springing to mind (2.1-2.2, 12.4, 22.4): kept in the claims' notes and the similes' points, not modelled as a claim. That the awakening factors are not obstacles, hindrances or corruptions of the mind (23.1): no pattern yet. The setting and Saṅgārava's going for refuge (1.1-1.3, 24.1-24.3)." ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-16"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.55/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

dho:t-sn46.6 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.6 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.6 Kuṇḍaliyasutta — Kuṇḍaliya"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:PartlyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "The fulfilment ladder (D20): sense restraint fulfils the three kinds of good conduct, which fulfil the four kinds of mindfulness meditation, which fulfil the seven awakening factors, which fulfil knowledge and freedom (2.1-2.8, with the closing line of each section: 5.7, 6.3, 7.6, 8.4). The three kinds of good conduct (6.2) and the four kinds of mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5) as lists. The stock formula (8.2-8.3)." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:leftOut "Kuṇḍaliya's framing (1.4-1.8). 'The benefit the Realized One lives for is the fruit of knowledge and freedom' (1.9): no pattern for what someone lives for. How each step is developed: sense restraint toward agreeable and disagreeable sights and the other senses (3.2-5.6), giving up bad conduct to develop good conduct (6.2), the observing practice of each mindfulness meditation (7.2-7.5): described, not modelled beyond the lists. Kuṇḍaliya's going for refuge (9.1-9.5)." ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-15"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.6/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

dho:t-sn46.3 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.3 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.3 Sīlasutta — Ethics"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:PartlyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "The awakening factors arising in sequence, each step a condition with its own words: recollecting and thinking about the teaching gives rise to mindfulness, then investigation, energy, rapture (not of the flesh), tranquility, immersion (with bliss) and equanimity (1.9-8.1); and the seven fruits and benefits they lead to, from enlightenment early in this life to heading upstream to the Akaniṭṭha realm (9.1-9.10)." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:leftOut "Why it matters to see, hear, approach, pay homage to, recollect and go forth following mendicants accomplished in ethics, immersion, knowledge, freedom, and the knowledge and vision of freedom: 'very helpful' (1.1-1.6); and living withdrawn in body and mind after hearing their teaching (1.8). Needs a pattern for what is called helpful, and the link from hearing to withdrawal is not modelled. Within the sequence, each factor is 'initiated', 'developed' and 'perfected' (e.g. 2.2-2.3); only its arising is modelled. Fruits 3-7 come 'with the ending of the five lower fetters' (9.5-9.9): kept in a note, not modelled." ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.3/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

dho:t-sn46.57 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.57 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.57 Aṭṭhikamahapphalasutta — A Skeleton"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:FullyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "The perception of a skeleton is very fruitful and beneficial (1.2, 1.6), developed together with the awakening factors (1.4), with the stock formula (1.5); one of two results, enlightenment in this very life or non-return; great benefit, great sanctuary from the yoke, great inspiration and great ease." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.57/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

dho:FullyEncoded a owl:Thing,
        dho:CoverageStatus ;
    rdfs:label "fully encoded"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Every teaching claim in the text is modelled."@en ;
    owl:sameAs dho:FullyEncoded .

dho:t-sn46.53 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.53 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.53 Aggisutta — Fire"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:PartlyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "The timing advice: which awakening factors it is the right or wrong time to develop when the mind is sluggish (4.1, 7.1) or restless (10.1, 13.1), and 'mindfulness is always useful' (15.4). The four fire similes, each illustrating three pieces of timing advice (5-6, 8-9, 11-12, 14-15; D22)." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:leftOut "The setting and the challenge from wanderers of other religions (1.1-3.1); the reason given for each piece of advice, that a sluggish mind is hard or easy to stimulate and a restless one hard or easy to settle (4.3, 7.3, 10.3, 13.3, repeated after each simile at 6.3, 9.3, 12.3, 15.3): cited, but no field records it. Corrected 2026-11-15: the encoded note had claimed the reasons (D19)." ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-13"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.53/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

dho:t-sn46.52 a owl:Thing,
        dho:Text ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    dct:source "SuttaCentral bilara-data: English by Bhikkhu Sujato, Pali Mahāsaṅgīti edition" ;
    owl:sameAs dho:t-sn46.52 ;
    skos:prefLabel "SN 46.52 Pariyāyasutta — Is There a Way?"@en ;
    dho:coverage dho:PartlyEncoded ;
    dho:encodes "The way in which the five hindrances become ten (9.1-9.12) and the seven awakening factors become fourteen (10.1-16.3): each of the twelve becomes twofold, as twelve divisions (D18) on four bases: internal and external (sensual desire, ill will, doubt, mindfulness, investigation, equanimity), physical and mental (energy, tranquility), with and without placing the mind (rapture, immersion), and the two qualities a compound name joins (dullness / drowsiness, restlessness / remorse)." ;
    dho:layer dho:SuttaLayer ;
    dho:leftOut "The narrative frame and the wanderers' challenge (1.1-7.2), including their restatement of the teaching as 'give up the five hindrances, corruptions of the heart that weaken wisdom' (3.2, 3.4, 6.1, 6.3): said by the interlocutors (D17), and the characterisation needs a pattern. The Buddha's counter-question to put to them, and that only the Realized One, his disciple or someone who heard it from them could answer it (8.1-8.6): no pattern for claims about who can answer." ;
    dho:modelledBy "project owner with Claude (AI assistant), from SuttaCentral's text" ;
    dho:modelledOn "2026-11-14"^^xsd:date ;
    dho:review dho:Unreviewed ;
    dho:url "https://suttacentral.net/sn46.52/en/sujato"^^xsd:anyURI .

